<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>High FIB-4 (3.43) with a mildly raised AST and low platelets at the yearly liver check in a 64-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, hypertension, low HDL, high triglycerides and obesity, who drinks little. Please stage his liver disease with elastography and advise on surveillance and treatment. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks, with elastography and an ultrasound the same day. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>How advanced is the fibrosis, and is this MASLD?</item><item>Does he need liver cancer surveillance, and how often?</item><item>Does he need screening for varices or a beta-blocker?</item><item>Which diabetes medicine would you favor for his liver, and are metformin and atorvastatin safe?</item><item>Which vaccines and other precautions do you advise?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Frazier, a 64-year-old former professional boxer, is seen at the request of Dr. Whitfield for a FIB-4 of 3.43 found at his yearly diabetes check. He feels well and has had no jaundice, abdominal or leg swelling, black stools, vomiting of blood or confusion. He drinks one or two beers on some weekends and has never drunk heavily. He has had diabetes for about twelve years and high blood pressure for many years. He has never had a blood transfusion or used injected drugs. He wants to keep working at his gym. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Joe Frazier was born on January 12, 1944, in Beaufort County, South Carolina, the eleventh child of a sharecropping family. [D]</item><item>As a teenager he moved to Philadelphia, where he had relatives, and worked in a slaughterhouse, where he punched sides of beef in the cold room. [D]</item><item>He won the heavyweight gold medal at the 1964 Olympic Games in Tokyo, fighting the final with a broken left thumb. [D]</item><item>Later in 1964 he learned that he had a cataract in his left eye; visually impaired, he turned professional anyway. The cataract grew worse, and by 1975, according to his eye doctor, his vision was 20/50 in the right eye and 20/400 in the left. [D]</item><item>He became undisputed world heavyweight champion in February 1970 and held the title until January 1973, defending it four times; he retired in 1976 after a professional record of 32 wins, 4 losses and 1 draw, with 27 knockouts, and returned for a single bout in 1981. [D]</item><item>His three-fight rivalry in the 1970s included a 1971 victory that handed his opponent his first professional defeat; in the third fight, in Manila in 1975, he could no longer see, and his trainer would not let him come out for the fifteenth round. [D]</item><item>In later life he lived in Philadelphia, where he owned and ran a boxing gym, and he had financial trouble. [D]</item><item>During his life it was reported publicly that he had diabetes and high blood pressure. [D]</item><item>He published an autobiography, Smokin&apos; Joe, in 1996. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

