<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (K76.0). Steatosis with diabetes, obesity, low HDL and high triglycerides and little alcohol meets the definition of MASLD. Other causes have been excluded by the tests above. [S]</item><item>Compensated cirrhosis (K74.69). A FIB-4 above 2.67 followed by a liver stiffness of 17.6 kPa, with platelets of 138, a nodular liver and a large spleen, is consistent with compensated advanced chronic liver disease, most likely cirrhosis. A biopsy would not change management. Child-Pugh A (5 points), MELD 8. There is no sign of decompensation. [S]</item><item>Liver cancer surveillance (K74.69). Cirrhosis from MASLD carries a risk of hepatocellular carcinoma high enough to justify surveillance by ultrasound with AFP every six months; today&apos;s baseline is clear and of good quality. Surveillance on time is what finds cancer when it can still be cured. [S]</item><item>Portal hypertension (K74.69). A stiffness between 15 and 25 kPa with platelets below 150 cannot rule clinically significant portal hypertension in or out, so an upper endoscopy to look for varices is advised; a beta-blocker would be started if varices needing treatment are found. [S]</item><item>Diabetes and the liver (E11.65). A GLP-1 receptor agonist such as semaglutide is favored: it lowers glucose and weight, has cardiovascular and kidney benefit, and improves steatohepatitis. Metformin is safe to continue in compensated cirrhosis with normal kidney function, and atorvastatin is safe and beneficial. Sulfonylureas and insulin are not needed and carry a risk of low blood sugar. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Whitfield&apos;s intake note and results: AST 48, ALT 42 U/L, alkaline phosphatase 96, albumin 4.0 g/dL, bilirubin 0.9 mg/dL, platelets 138, creatinine 1.08 mg/dL, FIB-4 3.43. Today: transient elastography with a median liver stiffness of 17.6 kPa (interquartile range 14%) and a controlled attenuation parameter of 318 dB/m; ultrasound with a coarse, echogenic, mildly nodular liver, a spleen of 13.4 cm, a patent portal vein, no ascites and no focal lesion; INR 1.1; AFP 4.2 ng/mL. Hepatitis B surface antigen, core antibody and surface antibody negative; hepatitis A antibody negative; hepatitis C antibody and HIV nonreactive; ferritin and transferrin saturation normal; antinuclear and smooth muscle antibodies negative. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Liver ultrasound with AFP every six months, starting with today&apos;s baseline. [S]</item><item>Upper endoscopy to screen for varices within the next six weeks; if none are found and the liver injury continues, repeat in two years. [S]</item><item>Start semaglutide and increase it as tolerated; continue metformin and atorvastatin 40 mg. [S]</item><item>Aim for 7 to 10% weight loss over a year. [S]</item><item>No alcohol at all; no ibuprofen or naproxen; acetaminophen no more than 2 g a day. [S]</item><item>Hepatitis A and B vaccine series and PCV20 now; influenza, COVID-19 and RSV vaccines in season. [S]</item><item>Albumin, bilirubin, platelets and kidney function every six months; repeat elastography in one year. [S]</item><item>Teach the warning signs of decompensation: abdominal or leg swelling, black stools or vomiting of blood, confusion, yellowing. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Whitfield, who saw him the same day. The diagnosis and plan were explained to Mr. Frazier, who agreed and asked for a written list of the warning signs, which was given. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Hepatology review of the six-month ultrasound and AFP, a visit with repeat elastography at one year, and sooner for any warning sign; Dr. Whitfield continues primary care. [S]</item></list></text>

